integers, not booleans | `True` is treated as line 1, `False` as line 0 |\n| 
Invalid type should be reported with clear error | Method may return incorrect 
result without warning |\n\n## Root Cause\nIn Python, `bool` is a subclass of 
`int` (`True == 1`, `False == 0`). The current code uses `isinstance(start, 
int)` to validate line numbers, which returns `True` for boolean values. This 
causes:\n1. `True` to be accepted as line 1 (valid but incorrect)\n2. `False` to
be accepted as line 0 (invalid but may pass validation depending on context)\n3.
Silent type coercion that hides bugs in calling code\n\n## Fix Plan\nReplace 
`isinstance(start, int)` with `type(start) is int` to reject boolean values 
while still accepting integer values. This ensures only actual `int` objects are
accepted, not `bool` subclasses.\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS'})
